<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Thomas Chittenden</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1730">1730</a>–<a href="/years/1797">1797</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Thomas Chittenden (1730–1797) was a farmer and political leader who served as governor of the Vermont Republic from 1778 to 1789 and again from 1790, and as the first governor of the state of Vermont after it joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> in 1791. During the Revolutionary War he negotiated with both Congress and the British to secure Vermont's independence from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thomas-chittenden.jpg" alt="Thomas Chittenden" width="320" height="389" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Simtropolitan · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:ThomasChittenden.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Thomas Chittenden was born in East Guilford in the Connecticut Colony on 6 January 1730. He lost an eye, and his opponents called him one eye Tom. On 4 October 1749 he married Elizabeth Meigs in Salisbury, Connecticut, and they had four sons and six daughters, all of whom survived to adulthood. He was a justice of the peace, sat in the Connecticut Colonial Assembly from 1765 to 1769 and served in Connecticut's 14th Regiment of Militia from 1767 to 1773, rising to colonel.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>With other residents of Salisbury he bought land from the Onion River Land Company of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethan-allen">Ethan Allen</a>, Ira Allen, Heman Allen and Remember Baker, and in 1774 he moved to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-hampshire-grants">New Hampshire Grants</a>, now Vermont, as the first settler of the town of Williston. He was a delegate to the convention of 1777 that drew up Vermont's constitution and its Declaration of Independence, and on 12 March 1778 the legislature chose him as governor, with Joseph Marsh as lieutenant governor.</p><p>During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-revolution">American Revolution</a> he sat on a committee empowered to negotiate Vermont's entry into the Union with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/continental-congress">Continental Congress</a>, which put the matter off so as not to antagonise <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-hampshire">New Hampshire</a>, both of which claimed the territory. He also took part in delicate talks with British authorities in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quebec">Quebec</a> about making Vermont a British province. Vermont was admitted in 1791 as the 14th state, and he remained governor until his death.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Chittenden died at Williston on 25 August 1797 and was buried in the cemetery there that bears his name. His epitaph reads: Out of storm and manifold perils rose an enduring state, the home of freedom and unity. A monument at the cemetery was begun in 1894 and dedicated in 1896. Frank Gaylord made bronze statues of him for the grounds of the State House at Montpelier and for Williston Central School, and Chittenden County and the town of Chittenden are named for him.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Thomas Chittenden” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Thomas Chittenden</b> served as governor almost throughout.</blockquote><a href="/state/vermont-republic">Vermont Republic</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Madison, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. Died in Montpelier, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.34,-72.63&amp;year=1773" aria-label="Madison on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Thomas Chittenden"><image href="/maps/globes/g300n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4196 3992h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4196 3992h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=41.34,-72.63&amp;year=1773">What was here in 1773? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
